  "subject": "🛠️ Axios Finish Line: Fix-it revolution",
  "date": "Thu, 05 Nov 2026 21:29:23 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🍽️ Plus: Kindness tab | Thursday, November 05, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 05, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 353 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: The great repair revival \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Americans are reviving the lost art of fixing their possessions — and pushing back against a culture of buying and tossing things.\n\n Repair Cafés are popping up in libraries and community centers across the country, where volunteers are helping people mend their broken toasters, busted zippers and family heirlooms for free. Why it matters: Most of us have too much stuff . Fixing what we own instead of throwing it away is a simple way to save money and cut waste.\n\n 🔎 Zoom in: In Houston , Axios' Shafaq Patel got her mom's sewing machine fixed at the local library's Repair Café. Volunteers told her that people have come in with jewelry, clocks, lamps, stuffed animals and more.\n\n In Des Moines , Axios' Linh Ta had a torn backpack strap stitched back together at another library Repair Café. In New Paltz, N.Y., volunteers rewired an antique fan, restored old family photos and replaced clasps on necklaces, AP reports . 🪛 Zoom out: In today's modern, disposable culture, many people have lost the ability to repair household goods, a skill that was once nearly universal, engineer Peter Counter told AP. He's studying Repair Cafés as part of a doctorate at the University for the Creative Arts in Farnham, England.\n\n \"The idea that you can fix your own stuff has receded because the skills are not being passed down. … If you want something fixed, it's almost certainly cheaper to go buy a new one.\" 🧰 Repair Cafés are helping preserve those disappearing skills.\n\n More than half of volunteers are over 60, according to a global survey , giving younger visitors a chance to learn from those who grew up fixing stuff. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🪖 Picking up the tab \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Natalie Peeples/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Tonight's dose of kindness comes from reader Billy Guice, who lives in Mississippi's Biloxi-Gulfport area, home to Keesler Air Force Base and the Naval Construction Battalion Center.\n\n \"When possible at lunch, I try to pick up the tab for a table of men and women in uniform, anonymously if possible. I ask that the establishment simply say, 'Thank you.'\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
