5510494\"><img src=\"https://link.axios.com/img/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94asr5yj.7j31/23e35892.gif\" alt=\"\" border=\"0\" /></a></td>\r\n<td style=\"padding-right:3.5%\" align=\"right\"> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2F4aW9zLWZpbmlzaC1saW5lLTc2YTlhM2VmLTE5ZTgtNDVhNS05NTNkLThjZGVmMWY3NTc5NS5odG1sP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB27b8e657\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"b\" style=\"color:#222;font-size:13px;font-family:'gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;text-decoration:underline\">View in browser</a> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"30\" style=\"height:30px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>  <tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.3\"> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"16\" style=\"height:16px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1\">\r\n<span class=\"s\" style=\"font-size:44px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#008dc8;letter-spacing:-1px;line-height:1\"> Axios Finish Line </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.5;padding-bottom:35px;padding-top:10px\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#222;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">By  Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei  <span style=\"margin:0 6px\">&middot;</span> Nov 05, 2026 </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.75;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;padding:0 3.5%\"><p><strong>Welcome back! </strong><em>Smart Brevity™ count: 353 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern.</em></p></td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: The great repair revival </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/C6UIXd1BLbx48bTW1MdJgc9WG7k=/0x0:1920x1080/1920x1080/2026/06/18/1781812709272.jpeg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of a woman leaning on a giant hammer\"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Americans are reviving</strong> the lost art of fixing their possessions — and pushing back against a culture of buying and tossing things.</p><ul><li><strong>Repair Cafés</strong> are popping up in libraries and community centers across the country, where volunteers are helping people mend their broken toasters, busted zippers and family heirlooms for free.</li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Most of us have <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjIvMDMvMTAvdG9vLW11Y2gtc3R1ZmYtc2VsZi1zdG9yYWdlLWZhY2lsaXRpZXM_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB142cf41d\" target=\"_blank\">too much stuff</a>. Fixing what we own instead of throwing it away is a simple way to save money and cut waste.</p><p>🔎<strong> Zoom in:</strong> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL2xvY2FsL2hvdXN0b24vMjAyNi8xMC8wMi9yZXBhaXItY2FmZS1oYXJyaXMtY291bnR5LXB1YmxpYy1saWJyYXJpZXM_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB25761a76\" target=\"_blank\">In Houston</a>, Axios' Shafaq Patel got her mom's sewing machine fixed at the local library's Repair Café. Volunteers told her that people have come in with jewelry, clocks, lamps, stuffed animals and more.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL2xvY2FsL2Rlcy1tb2luZXMvMjAyNC8xMi8wNC9kZXMtbW9pbmVzLXJlcGFpci1jYWZlLWZpeC1mcmVlLWxpYnJhcnk_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBbc5c5f9b\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>In Des Moines</strong></a><strong>,</strong> Axios' Linh Ta had a torn backpack strap stitched back together at another library Repair Café.</li><li><strong>In New Paltz, N.Y.,</strong> volunteers rewired an antique fan, restored old family photos and replaced clasps on necklaces, <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly9hcG5ld3MuY29tL2FydGljbGUvcmVwYWlyLWNhZmVzLWVjb25vbXktYW50aWNvbnN1bWVyaXNtLWFmZm9yZGFiaWxpdHktYnV5LW5vdGhpbmctZDNhY2FjM2VjMmFhZTVlODUyOTRiMzRmMGY0NzY0Yjg_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB7ba822b0\" target=\"_blank\"><em>AP reports</em></a><em>.</em></li></ul><p><strong>🪛 Zoom out:</strong> In today's modern, disposable culture, many people have lost the ability to repair household goods, a skill that was once nearly universal, engineer Peter Counter told AP. He's studying Repair Cafés as part of a doctorate at the University for the Creative Arts in Farnham, England.</p><ul><li><strong>\"The idea that you</strong> can fix your own stuff has receded because the skills are not being passed down. … If you want something fixed, it's almost certainly cheaper to go buy a new one.\"</li></ul><p><strong>🧰 Repair Cafés are helping</strong> preserve those disappearing skills.</p><ul><li><strong>More than half</strong> of volunteers are over 60, according to a <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucmVwYWlyY2FmZS5vcmcvZW4vcmVwYWlyLWNhZmVzLWF0dHJhY3QtMS01LW1pbGxpb24tdmlzaXRvcnMtZXZlcnkteWVhci8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB9a4a583d\" target=\"_blank\">global survey</a>, giving younger visitors a chance to learn from those who grew up fixing stuff.</li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtNzZhOWEzZWYtMTllOC00NWE1LTk1M2QtOGNkZWYxZjc1Nzk1Lmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBbda4b7a2\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.351325/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Tweet this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48296683.\" style=\"display:inline-blo